#
# ## fz-kdt.119 re-pricing
#
# `budget.codegen.functions` is `fz.codegen.define_function.stop` from
# `fz2 --emit=stats run <this fixture>`. It moved 32 -> 46 at fz-kdt.119, and
# every one of the fourteen is a `Kernel.dbg/1` or `Kernel.fz_dbg_value/1`
# specialization that BECAME LIVE: the argument's type is a union of
# `{:unknown | any}` at each of three positions, the tuple test used to see
# only "a 3-tuple" and collapsed the eight clauses into one destination, and a
# per-position test tells them apart, so eight arms are minted where one was.
# That 2^arity product is a real cost for a callee that reads no position,
# and fz-kdt.140 owns it.
#
# The number was 26 before this landing and 32 was already the truth, so the
# header was stale by six before anything here touched it. The other nine
# `budget.*` keys on this fixture have no producer anywhere in compiler2 --
# they name a "planner" that does not exist -- and fz-kdt.139 owns removing
# them corpus-wide.
#
# # receive_map_pattern
#
# fz-puj.47 (X6) — receive matcher implementing Pattern::Map via PerRow.
#
# Map patterns route through PerRow (no Switch specialisation), so the
# matcher dispatches via `fz_matcher_map_get(map, key) -> u64` per entry.
# A NIL_BITS return signals miss; otherwise the lookup result recurses
# through compile_pattern against the value pattern.
#
# This fixture uses `%{name: n}` against incoming maps; the value
# position binds `n` to the atom under the `name` key. Interp mirrors
# via direct heap-walk of the map's pair array.
#
# Float and Binary map-key patterns surface a clean CodegenError pointing
# at a follow-up — encoding them would require a per-receive startup
# allocation pass (heap-typed RHS).
fn main() do
  send(self(), %{name: :alice, age: 30})
  send(self(), %{name: :bob, age: 42})
  send(self(), :stop)

  a = receive do
    %{name: n} -> n
    _ -> :unknown
  end

  b = receive do
    %{name: n} -> n
    _ -> :unknown
  end

  c = receive do
    %{name: n} -> n
    _ -> :unknown
  end

  dbg({a, b, c})
end
